From: nightcarnage <nightcarnage@bc250-project>
Subject: [PATCH 23/23] drm/amdgpu: fix GB_ADDR_CONFIG for BC-250/gfx1013

The stock golden register sets mmGB_ADDR_CONFIG to 0x00000044 which sets
NUM_SHADER_ENGINES to 0. The BC-250 community reports 0x00100044 as
required for ROCm on this board — bit 20 changes NUM_SHADER_ENGINES from
0 to 2.

GB_ADDR_CONFIG controls pipe interleave and shader-engine address swizzle.
A wrong shader-engine count causes distinct GPU addresses to collide on the
same physical location, which exactly matches the measured GPU aliasing
signature (docs/17 in GabriWar's analysis):
- Two live hipMalloc buffers alias despite disjunct VAs
- CPU never affected (doesn't go through the shader-engine swizzle)
- Bimodal per-process (swizzle is static per VM config)

This is the community's leading hypothesis for the root cause of the GPU
corruption on BC-250. The change is one register value. It matters more if
the 40-CU unlock is active, since that changes the harvest configuration
this register describes.

UNTESTED — flagged as a lead by GabriWar. Build and A/B test before
declaring victory.

Signed-off-by: nightcarnage
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/soc15.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/soc15.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/soc15.c
index 1234567..89abcde 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/soc15.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/amd/amdgpu/soc15.c
@@ -1652,7 +1652,7 @@ static void soc15_program_asd(struct amdgpu_device *adev)
 	SOC15_REG_GOLDEN_VALUE(GC, 0, mmGB_ADDR_CONFIG, 0x0c1800ff, 0x00000044),
+	SOC15_REG_GOLDEN_VALUE(GC, 0, mmGB_ADDR_CONFIG, 0x0c1800ff, 0x00100044),
